(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-antibacterials-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antibacterials Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which penicillin dosage form is given intramuscularly as a single long-acting dose?","choices":["Penicillin G (benzylpenicillin)","Ampicillin sodium","Penicillin G benzathine (Bicillin)","Penicillin V potassium (Pen VK)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The benzathine salt is the depot form, which is why one injection can cover an indication such as syphilis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which antibiotic classes bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, and which bind the 30S?","choices":["50S — vancomycin, daptomycin; 30S — linezolid, tigecycline","50S — macrolides, clindamycin, chloramphenicol, streptogramins; 30S — aminoglycosides, tetracyclines","50S — aminoglycosides, tetracyclines; 30S — macrolides, clindamycin, chloramphenicol, streptogramins","50S — fluoroquinolones, metronidazole; 30S — penicillins, cephalosporins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The bacterial ribosome is 70S against the mammalian 80S, which is the basis for selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"For a time-dependent agent with no post-antibiotic effect, what dosing strategy does the lecture recommend?","choices":["Alternate-day dosing to allow tissue concentrations to accumulate between doses","Continuous or frequent infusions, keeping the concentration above the minimal inhibitory concentration for 40 to 70 percent of the dosing interval","A single large daily dose, taking advantage of the prolonged post-antibiotic effect","A loading dose followed by widely spaced maintenance doses, with the interval guided by trough levels alone rather than by time above the minimal inhibitory concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Without a post-antibiotic effect, organisms resume growing as soon as the level falls, so time above the threshold is what matters. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How do levofloxacin and moxifloxacin differ in coverage?","choices":["Moxifloxacin requires renal adjustment; levofloxacin does not","Levofloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; moxifloxacin does not","Moxifloxacin has Pseudomonas coverage; levofloxacin does not","Levofloxacin covers atypicals; moxifloxacin does not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both improve on ciprofloxacin's Gram-positive activity and both cover community-acquired pneumonia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which atypical organisms do macrolides cover?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Acinetobacter species, Burkholderia cepacia and Stenotrophomonas maltophilia","Bacteroides fragilis and Clostridium perfringens","Rickettsiae, Borrelia burgdorferi and Brucella","Legionella species, Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Chlamydophila pneumoniae and Chlamydia trachomatis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and this atypical coverage is what makes them a community-acquired pneumonia agent. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why did aminoglycoside dosing move from every 8 hours to every 24 hours?","choices":["Once-daily dosing eliminates the need for therapeutic drug monitoring","Renal clearance is so rapid that more frequent dosing has no effect","They show a post-antibiotic effect, so killing persists after the level falls, and concentration-dependent killing favours a large single dose","They show time-dependent killing, so a longer interval keeps the level above the minimal inhibitory concentration for a greater share of the dosing period"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the clearest clinical application of the concentration-dependent pattern from earlier in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What is the defining clinical advantage of aztreonam?","choices":["No cross-reactivity with beta-lactams, so it can be used in truly penicillin-allergic patients","The broadest spectrum of any beta-lactam, covering Gram-positives, Gram-negatives and anaerobes","Oral bioavailability allowing outpatient completion of therapy","Reliable central nervous system penetration for meningitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That property is what makes a monobactam worth having despite its narrow Gram-negative-only spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is daptomycin's mechanism, and what is its single most important limitation?","choices":["It inhibits protein synthesis at the 50S ribosomal subunit, and it cannot be used in renal failure","It inhibits cell wall cross-linking, and it cannot be used in penicillin allergy","It inhibits DNA gyrase, and it cannot be used under 18 years of age","It causes bacterial depolarization inhibiting DNA, RNA and protein synthesis, and it cannot be used in pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Daptomycin is inactivated by pulmonary surfactant, so the lung is the one site where it fails. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which tetracycline precautions relate to children and pregnancy?","choices":["Discoloration of teeth and depression of skeletal growth — avoid under 8 years old and in the second and third trimesters","Cartilage damage — avoid under 18 years old","Kernicterus from bilirubin displacement — avoid in the first 30 days of life and throughout the third trimester of pregnancy","Grey baby syndrome — avoid in all trimesters"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both follow from the drug binding calcium in developing bone and teeth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which adverse reactions does the lecture list for the antipseudomonal penicillins?","choices":["Hypersensitivity reaction, rash, and diarrhoea","Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and red man syndrome","Tendon rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and central nervous system toxicity","Disulfiram-like reaction with ethanol and metallic taste"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with monitoring for anaphylaxis, renal function, complete blood count and bleeding. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What monitoring does the lecture specify for daptomycin?","choices":["Muscle pain and creatine phosphokinase levels","Platelet count, because of thrombocytopenia","Trough concentrations drawn before the fourth dose","Liver function tests and bilirubin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The muscle toxicity is what the monitoring is aimed at. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which indications are given for cefazolin?","choices":["Surgical prophylaxis, methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus infection, and urinary tract infection","Neutropenic fever and hospital-acquired pneumonia","Meningitis and neonatal sepsis","Complicated intra-abdominal infection with Bacteroides fragilis, anaerobic abscess and biliary sepsis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin covers a similar niche orally, aimed at cellulitis and urinary tract infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Ampicillin and amoxicillin are the drugs of choice for which set of indications?","choices":["Syphilis, gas gangrene, and meningococcal disease","Enterococcus, Listeria, endocarditis prophylaxis, upper respiratory tract infection, and community-acquired pneumonia at high dose","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia and osteomyelitis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa pneumonia, nosocomial intra-abdominal infection, and polymicrobial infection in the critically ill patient"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminopenicillins add some Gram-negative aerobic activity to the natural penicillin spectrum but still lack Staphylococcus coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient taking an aminopenicillin is also on warfarin. What does the lecture identify as the interaction?","choices":["No clinically meaningful interaction, since penicillins are renally cleared","Increased risk of tendon rupture when the two are combined","Abnormal prolongation of the prothrombin time","Reduced anticoagulant effect requiring an increased warfarin dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists anticoagulants first among the aminopenicillin interactions. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Clindamycin is the drug of choice for which situations?","choices":["Bacterial meningitis, cerebral abscess and ventriculitis requiring reliable central nervous system penetration, together with neurosurgical prophylaxis after craniotomy","Toxin-mediated disease, skin and soft tissue infection, osteomyelitis, surgical prophylaxis in penicillin allergy, and as part of intra-abdominal combination therapy","Pseudomonal pneumonia and febrile neutropenia","Urinary tract infection and pyelonephritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The toxin-mediated indication follows from inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis and therefore toxin production. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What distinguishes a bacteriostatic drug from a bactericidal drug, as the lecture defines them?","choices":["Bacteriostatic requires a higher concentration to act; bactericidal acts at any concentration above zero","Bacteriostatic agents are given orally and bactericidal agents are given intravenously","Bacteriostatic inhibits a vital growth pathway without directly causing death; bactericidal disrupts function enough that death occurs","Bacteriostatic works only on Gram-positive organisms; bactericidal works on both Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's split is about whether the drug itself kills, not about how fast it works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What monitoring does the lecture specify for the penicillinase-resistant penicillins?","choices":["Serum drug troughs drawn before the fourth dose","Creatine phosphokinase and muscle pain","Hepatic function, complete blood count, and signs or symptoms of anaphylaxis","Renal function alone, since the class is cleared entirely by the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Note the dosing consideration for this class is hepatic rather than renal function, unlike most other penicillins. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How do sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim block folate synthesis at two different steps?","choices":["Sulfamethoxazole inhibits the conversion of dihydrofolic acid to tetrahydrofolic acid via dihydrofolate reductase; trimethoprim inhibits the conversion of para-aminobenzoic acid to dihydrofolic acid via tetrahydropteroic acid synthetase","Both inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, at different binding sites","Sulfamethoxazole blocks folate absorption from the gut; trimethoprim blocks its renal reabsorption","Sulfamethoxazole inhibits conversion of para-aminobenzoic acid to dihydrofolic acid via tetrahydropteroic acid synthetase; trimethoprim inhibits conversion of dihydrofolic acid to tetrahydrofolic acid via dihydrofolate reductase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two sequential steps in one pathway, which is what makes the combination synergistic. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the approximate rate of penicillin cross-sensitivity with cephalosporins in this lecture?","choices":["Cross-sensitivity does not occur at all between the two classes","Less than 1 percent","Approximately 10 percent","Approximately 25 percent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The figure matters clinically because a reported penicillin allergy does not automatically exclude a cephalosporin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Under drug factors for choosing a regimen, which toxicities does the lecture list as the organ systems to consider?","choices":["Cardiac, pulmonary, endocrine, musculoskeletal, ocular, and dermatologic systems","Gastrointestinal, renal, hepatic, ocular, reproductive, immunologic","Hematologic, renal, hepatic, endocrine, vascular, lymphatic","Gastrointestinal, integument, hematologic, central nervous system, hepatic, renal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Six systems, and they map onto the monitoring parameters repeated for individual classes later in the lecture. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why is polymyxin resistance uncommon, and why is use expected to increase?","choices":["The mechanism is impossible for bacteria to circumvent, and newer formulations have removed the nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity entirely","It is used only topically, so systemic resistance cannot emerge","It is always combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor that prevents resistance","The class went largely unused for the last 50 years, and rising multidrug resistance is bringing it back despite optimal regimens not being well studied"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low historical exposure is the reason resistance never developed widely. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Cefepime is the drug of choice for which infections?","choices":["Surgical prophylaxis and uncomplicated cystitis","Neutropenic fever, nosocomial infections including hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia, and pseudomonal infections","Community-acquired pneumonia and skin and soft tissue infection in the outpatient setting, together with uncomplicated urinary tract infection","Complicated intra-abdominal and complicated urinary tract infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It has Gram-negative and antipseudomonal activity plus retained Gram-positive activity, though no methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, Enterococcus or anaerobic coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What must a patient starting metronidazole be counselled to avoid, and why?","choices":["Tyramine-containing foods, because of hypertensive crisis","Sun exposure, because of photosensitivity","Ethanol, because of a disulfiram-like reaction","Dairy products, because of chelation reducing absorption"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the class's signature counselling point, alongside headache, nausea and vomiting. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which organism has increasingly rendered the penicillinase-resistant penicillins ineffective, and what replaces them?","choices":["Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, for which vancomycin is the treatment of choice","Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus, for which linezolid is the treatment of choice","Extended-spectrum beta-lactamase producing organisms, for which carbapenems are preferred","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, for which cefepime is preferred"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The class was built for Staphylococcus aureus and methicillin resistance is precisely what defeats it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which linezolid interactions can produce serotonin syndrome?","choices":["Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tyramine-containing foods, and pseudoephedrine","Warfarin, phenytoin and digoxin","Iron, calcium and dairy products","Ethanol, which produces a disulfiram-like reaction with flushing and vomiting"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and platelets are monitored separately because of thrombocytopenia. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which carbapenem lacks Pseudomonas aeruginosa coverage?","choices":["Meropenem","Imipenem","Doripenem","Ertapenem"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The class covers Pseudomonas as a rule, and ertapenem is the stated exception. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"How do macrolides interact with other drugs, and in what order of severity?","choices":["They displace substrates from plasma protein binding sites","Metabolites bind CYP3A subclass enzymes forming an inactive complex, raising levels of substrates such as carbamazepine, cyclosporine, digoxin, midazolam and theophylline, with erythromycin greater than clarithromycin greater than azithromycin","They induce CYP3A subclass enzymes, lowering levels of substrates such as carbamazepine, cyclosporine, digoxin, midazolam and theophylline, with azithromycin greater than clarithromycin greater than erythromycin","They chelate divalent cations, reducing absorption of co-administered drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ranking is the practical point: azithromycin is the one to reach for when interactions matter. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which toxicities define the aminoglycoside class?","choices":["Hepatotoxicity and thrombocytopenia","Tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy","Serotonin syndrome, hypertensive crisis, and thrombocytopenia","Renal toxicity and ototoxicity, requiring renal dose adjustment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and vancomycin shares both, which is why the combination is watched carefully. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does vancomycin inhibit cell wall synthesis, and how does this differ from a penicillin?","choices":["It binds the two D-alanine residues on the peptide to block cross-linking, rather than inactivating the cross-linking enzyme","It inactivates the penicillin-binding protein by forming a covalent bond at its active site, exactly as a penicillin does","It inhibits the 50S ribosomal subunit and blocks transpeptidation","It disrupts the outer membrane by displacing magnesium and calcium ions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin mimics the substrate and disables the enzyme; vancomycin covers the substrate itself, which is why resistance is harder to develop. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which cephalosporin provides methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage?","choices":["Ceftolozane/tazobactam, a fifth generation agent","Ceftriaxone, a third generation agent","Ceftaroline, a fifth generation agent","Cefepime, a fourth generation agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the cephalosporin that breaks the class's usual absence of methicillin-resistant coverage, and it must be renally adjusted. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 37"}]};
